/-!
## Section 5: Computational Verification (Partial)

Concrete examples of arithmetic knots and their invariants,
verifying the dictionary for small primes.
-/

/-- The trefoil knot as an arithmetic knot: p = 2.
    The trefoil has genus 1 and Alexander polynomial t² - t + 1.
    The Iwasawa polynomial for p = 2 over ℚ(μ₂) is 1 (trivial).
    This discrepancy illustrates the limits of the dictionary. -/
def trefoilArithmeticKnot : ArithmeticKnotZ := ⟨2, Nat.prime_two⟩

/-- Example: linking of primes 5 and 11.
    (5/11) = 1 since 5⁵ ≡ 1 (mod 11). These primes are "linked". -/
example : (5 : ℕ) ^ ((11 - 1) / 2) % 11 = 1 := by norm_num

/-- Example: linking of primes 3 and 7.
    (3/7) = -1 since 3³ ≡ -1 (mod 7). These primes are "unlinked". -/
example : (3 : ℕ) ^ ((7 - 1) / 2) % 7 ≠ 1 := by norm_num

/-!
## Summary

This formalization captures the key definitions of the arithmetic topology
approach to BSD:

1. Arithmetic knots ↔ prime ideals (Mazur dictionary)
2. Arithmetic linking number ↔ Legendre symbol (quadratic reciprocity)
3. Arithmetic Thurston norm → rank bounds (speculative)
4. Arithmetic Turaev torsion → BSD regulator (speculative)

The first two are rigorous and well-established. The last two are the
novel contributions of this research direction and remain conjectural.
All non-trivial proofs use `sorry`, indicating the speculative nature
of the arithmetic Thurston norm and Turaev torsion definitions.
-/
